For every abstract that contains a sentence relevant to this claim, return one record:
- pmid;
- quote: one or two consecutive sentences copied exactly, character for character, from that abstract;
- quote_role: finding (a result of this study) | background (stated as known, cited, or a review summarising others) | speculation (may, might, could, suggests, we propose);
- stance: supports | refutes | neither (for this claim, in this context). "refutes" only when the finding says the relation itself does not hold (no effect, or the opposite effect); a finding about consequences, side effects or other relations of the same entities is "neither"; a finding for an intervention of the same kind as the claim's (another exercise programme, another training course, another supplement of the same kind, another drug of the same class) on the claim's outcome is still "supports" or "refutes", with intervention_match "class" or "related_compound": code gives it less weight, so do not drop it as "neither"; the same holds for a population other than the claim's (qualifiers_match population "no");
- species: the organism the quoted sentence is about (a sentence about other organisms does not support a claim about this one);
- endpoint: what the finding measures: lifespan | healthspan | biomarker | disease | mechanism | unknown;
- effect: for a lifespan finding, the direction the study found: increase | decrease | no_effect (no significant change); for other endpoints "unknown";
- system: in_vivo | ex_vivo | in_vitro; study_type: primary_experiment | rct | open_label_pilot | observational | review | other;
- sex: the sex in which the finding was observed: male | female | both | unknown (not stated);
- strain: the strain or stock as the abstract names it (e.g. "C57BL/6J", "UM-HET3"), else "";
- dose, age_at_start: as the abstract states them (e.g. "14 ppm in food", "600 days"), else "";
- human_rungs: for a finding in humans, which kind of measure it reports: "human_markers" (laboratory and instrumental measures: blood markers, body composition, imaging, ageing clocks) and/or "human_clinical" (function, frailty, pain, disease, mortality); empty for other organisms;
- effect_percent: % change of lifespan if the abstract states it, else null;
- qualifiers_match: for each of species, sex, age_at_start, dose, comparator, population that the abstract reports: "yes" (matches the claim), "no" (contradicts, e.g. a disease population for healthy adults, ex vivo for an in vivo claim) or "n/a" (the claim does not specify it). Leave out a key the abstract does not report; do not guess. Strain is compared by code;
- intervention_match: exact | dose_differs (same compound and schedule, another dose) | regimen_differs (another route or schedule: in food or water vs a weekly dose, continuous vs intermittent) | related_compound | class. Judge it at the level at which the CLAIM names the intervention: when the claim names a kind of intervention ("resistance training", "power training", "adequate protein intake", "a home-based exercise programme", "a probiotic"), any study of that kind is "exact"; "class" only when the claim names a specific product, compound or programme (a named device, "squat exercise", "Otago programme", "metformin") and the study used another member of the same kind; a multicomponent programme that contains the claim's intervention is "class" when the claim's part cannot be read on its own;
- program: "ITP" or "CITP" if the study is from such a multi-site program, else "";
- flags: any of conflict_of_interest, small_n, open_label, subgroup_only, preprint, short_lived_controls.
Method v3 fields: read the WHOLE abstract for these, not only the quoted sentence:
- design: rct (only when the abstract says the allocation was random: "randomised", "randomized", "randomly assigned") | non_randomised (controlled but not randomised, or before-after in one group; a placebo-controlled or double-blind study that does not say it was randomised, and groups formed by choice, are non_randomised) | observational | animal | cells | review | meta_analysis | other. A review or commentary is "review" even when the sentence restates one trial (name the trial in reports_trial);
- n_total: the number of participants (or animals) the finding comes from, as an integer, or null if the abstract does not state it as one number. Do not add up group sizes; when the finding comes from a subset whose size is not stated (e.g. the patients with the condition among those enrolled), n_total is null. A number written in words counts ("Forty-two older adults" = 42). n_quote: the shortest phrase of the abstract, copied exactly, that gives this number ("" if null). Code checks that the phrase is in the abstract;
- comparison: between_groups (treated vs control or placebo) | within_group (before vs after in the same people) | none;
- effect_reported: never leave it empty when the abstract gives any number for that outcome (a difference in units with its confidence interval, a percent change, a ratio, a standardised difference, a before-after pair). The shortest phrase of the abstract, copied exactly, that gives the size of the effect for the outcome closest to the claim (the claim's object; when the claim or the hypothesis names several outcomes, the first one named that the abstract reports): a ratio with its interval ("OR 2.1 (95% CI 1.3-3.4)"), a percent change ("12% increase"), a standardised difference ("d = 0.45"), a difference in units ("+1.8 kg", "35±9 m"), or the authors' word for the size ("a modest improvement"). Whenever the abstract gives any number for that outcome's effect, copy it; "" only when it gives none (a p value alone is not a size);
- blinding: of the study the finding comes from: double_blind | single_blind | assessor_blind (outcome assessors blinded, participants not) | open (the abstract says open-label, unblinded or single-arm) | not_reported (a trial or controlled study whose abstract does not say) | not_applicable (observational, animal, cell, review or meta-analysis);
- outcome_subjective: true when the outcome of the quote is reported or rated by the participant (a questionnaire, quality of life, pain, fatigue, mood, perceived exertion or effort), false when it is measured (a performance test, strength, a biomarker, imaging, events, mortality), null when unclear;
- effect_class: none (the result for that same outcome is no effect, or not significant) | present (any other result). A null result on another outcome of the abstract does not make it "none". Code computes small, medium or large from effect_reported;
- reports_trial: when the quoted sentence of a review or commentary restates the result of one specific trial, the trial as named in the abstract (e.g. "the ABC trial"), else "". Such a sentence keeps quote_role "background" but must still be returned, with the stance of that trial's result.
Trials and studies with several outcomes: judge the claim by the primary outcome. A result reported only for one sex, one dose arm or another subset of the participants is flagged "subgroup_only". If the primary outcome was null and only secondary outcomes or subgroups improved, return two records from that abstract: the null primary outcome with stance "refutes", and the positive secondary or subgroup result with stance "supports" and flag "subgroup_only".
Skip abstracts with nothing relevant. Return an empty list if none is relevant. Do not use knowledge outside these abstracts.

